+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// What this was, and why a wheel is the wrong control for it
+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+//
+// wxChoice was a UIPickerView: the spinning drum iOS uses for a date, embedded
+// in the layout where the control goes. It works, in the sense that a choice
+// can be made with it, and it is wrong in every other way.
+//
+// A UIPickerView is 216 points tall and it does not shrink. A wxChoice on a
+// dialog is one line high and sits in a row with a label and whatever comes
+// after it, so what a sizer got was a control sixteen times the height it had
+// asked for - a wheel filling the dialog, with the rest of the page pushed off
+// the bottom of it. A drum is also not what this platform uses for a short
+// list of named things any more: since UIButton learned to show a UIMenu, a
+// fixed set of choices is a pull-down button, with a tick against the one in
+// force and the list opening under the finger that asked for it.
+//
+// So it is that button. The height is the height of a button, the list is the
+// system's own, and nothing about wxChoice's interface changes: the peer still
+// answers GetValue()/SetValue() with an index, and still sends wxEVT_CHOICE
+// through SendSelectionChangedEvent() when a row is chosen.
 
